{"data": {"id": "10.5281/zenodo.23471990", "type": "dois", "attributes": {"doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23471990", "prefix": "10.5281", "suffix": "zenodo.23471990", "identifiers": [], "alternateIdentifiers": [], "creators": [{"name": "Keshav, Kaushik", "nameType": "Personal", "givenName": "Kaushik", "familyName": "Keshav", "nameIdentifiers": [], "affiliation": []}], "titles": [{"title": "Code for: Threshold Transfer and Metric Inflation in Industrial Control System Anomaly Detection"}], "publisher": "Zenodo", "container": {}, "publicationYear": 2026, "subjects": [], "contributors": [], "dates": [{"date": "2026-10-19", "dateType": "Issued"}, {"date": "2026-10-19", "dateType": "Created"}], "language": null, "types": {"ris": "COMP", "bibtex": "misc", "citeproc": "article", "schemaOrg": "SoftwareSourceCode", "resourceType": "", "resourceTypeGeneral": "Software"}, "relatedIdentifiers": [{"relationType": "HasVersion", "relatedIdentifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23471991", "relatedIdentifierType": "DOI"}], "relatedItems": [], "sizes": [], "formats": [], "version": null, "rightsList": [{"rights": "Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International", "rightsUri": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode", "schemeUri": "https://spdx.org/licenses/", "rightsIdentifier": "cc-by-4.0", "rightsIdentifierScheme": "SPDX"}], "descriptions": [{"description": "This archive contains the complete, reproducible source code accompanying the article \u201cThreshold Transfer and Metric Inflation in Industrial Control System Anomaly Detection: A Measurement Study and a Physics-Grounded Baseline\u201d. The code regenerates every table and figure reported in the paper from the two public HAI industrial control system security datasets, releases 20.07 and 21.03. The package loads each release, removes constant channels, derives the labelled attack events, and evaluates six anomaly detectors: a Gaussian conditional residual, a temporal vector-autoregressive residual, PCA reconstruction error, a per-sensor z-score, a control-loop residual built on the plant\u2019s physical process relationships, and a fused detector. A uniform random detector is included as a diagnostic baseline.\n\nThe study quantifies two evaluation problems. The first is metric inflation: under point-adjusted F1, a detector that emits uniform random scores reaches 0.9106 on HAI 20.07 and 0.7335 on HAI 21.03, against honest point-wise F1 scores of 0.0668 and 0.0404. The code reproduces this effect, compares it against a closed-form event-hit probability, and evaluates point-wise, event-wise, point-adjusted and PA%K protocols. It also reports false-alarm time, event recall, detection latency, per-process recall, bootstrap confidence intervals, a smoothing ablation and an alarm-budget sweep. The second problem is threshold transfer: a threshold calibrated on one plant release drives every model-based detector into a permanent alarm state on the other. The code measures this failure and tests label-free recalibration on normal data from the target release.\n\nThe repository contains the Python package (data loading, detectors, metrics, pipeline and experiment definitions), a script that runs all experiments, a verification script that compares regenerated values with those printed in the paper, a script that regenerates the paper\u2019s figures, a multi-seed analysis of the random baseline, the CSV result tables, unit tests, and documentation of the reproduction settings. The HAI datasets themselves are not redistributed here. They are publicly available and must be downloaded separately (instructions and a helper script are included). Please cite the original HAI dataset publications and follow their licence terms.\n\nTo reproduce the results, install the dependencies listed in requirements.txt (Python 3, NumPy, pandas, SciPy, scikit-learn, Matplotlib), place the downloaded datasets in the data folder, and run the main script followed by the verification script. Most regenerated values match the paper exactly or to rounding. Entries that depend on a single random draw of the random detector use fixed seeds, which are documented in the reproduction notes together with the spread observed over 50 independent draws. The software is released under the MIT licence.", "descriptionType": "Abstract"}], "geoLocations": [], "fundingReferences": [], "xml": "", "url": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.23471990", "contentUrl": null, "metadataVersion": 1, "schemaVersion": "http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4", "source": "api", "isActive": true, "state": "findable", "reason": null, "viewCount": 0, "viewsOverTime": [], "downloadCount": 0, "downloadsOverTime": [], "referenceCount": 0, "citationCount": 0, "citationsOverTime": [], "partCount": 0, "partOfCount": 0, "versionCount": 1, "versionOfCount": 0, "created": "2026-10-19T10:53:01.000Z", "registered": "2026-10-19T10:53:01.000Z", "published": "2026", "updated": "2026-10-19T10:55:20.000Z"}, "relationships": {"client": {"data": {"id": "cern.zenodo", "type": "clients"}}, "provider": {"data": {"id": "cern", "type": "providers"}}, "media": {"data": {"id": "10.5281/zenodo.23471990", "type": "media"}}, "references": {"data": []}, "citations": {"data": []}, "parts": {"data": []}, "partOf": {"data": []}, "versions": {"data": [{"id": "10.5281/zenodo.23471991", "type": "dois"}]}, "versionOf": {"data": []}}}}